From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: xob0t <32616886+xob0t@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 05:28:40 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 13/22] Base constructor calls put back where the compiler left
 them out or pasted them in

A constructor that runs no constructor of its base class is not valid IL (ILVerify:
ThisUninitReturn), yet that is how one reads back when the C++ compiler inlined the
base constructor: an empty one (MonoBehaviour's, object's) leaves nothing behind, a
small one leaves its statements in the middle of the constructor.

New pass BaseConstructorCalls, run on the finished method before InitializerOrder:

- where the statements of a straight-line constructor of the base class stand in the
  constructor (with values in place of its parameters; plain field stores may stand
  between plain stores), they become a call of it again;
- otherwise a constructor of the base class whose code does nothing (read back, and
  the same for the ones it calls) is called after the field initializers; parameters
  such a constructor never reads are given default values (HMethod.Notes says so);
- otherwise the place gets an Effect marker ("base constructor call not recovered").

REWIND_NO_BASE_CONSTRUCTOR_CALLS turns it off, REWIND_BASE_CONSTRUCTOR_TRACE prints
what was done per constructor.

Unity 6000.0 Master x64 game, three game assemblies: ThisUninitReturn 114 -> 1 (in a
constructor that has a gap and now a marker); 103 calls inserted, 27 put back, 5
marked. Complete bodies unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
